#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Report where the live factory install differs from the repository.

Compares the worker profile, the plugins in the root and worker homes, and the
webhook config against a commit of the repository, read with git plumbing so
the checkout's working tree and branch do not matter. Also checks that the
dashboard's OIDC sign-in can keep a user signed in, that the gateway rejected
no webhook delivery with 429 in the last hour, and that the owned-refs file
the `github` route reads is present and fresh. Silent when the install
matches. Prints one line per difference and exits 1, or exits 2 when it cannot
run.

It also checks that the config keys, cron jobs, and launchd services the setup
runbook creates still match what it creates (the CONFIG, CRON, and SERVICES
tables), that a cron job delivers only to a platform with a home channel in
its profile and that its last run did not fail, naming the error with secrets
masked, that the root home's cron wrappers match the
repository, that the worker home's secrets.command is the worker helper
whenever a proxy token is configured, that the worker's .env holds no secret
only the gateway may hold, and that each configured repository checkout exists
and has origin/HEAD set, and that the root home's broker socket answers a
status request and reports the upload token, the Linear app credentials, the
proxy token, and the token of each server in claude_session.mcp_servers
present. That check sends only {"op": "status"}, never uploads or asks for a
token, and prints no value. For each configured repository, it runs the
installed claude-session --mcp-status and reports each MCP server that is not
connected and not in claude_session.disabled_mcp_servers. For each root-home mcp_servers entry that is
not enabled: false, it runs hermes mcp test with the root home and reports a server that does not connect,
one whose sampling.enabled is not false, and one whose header value is anything but one ${VAR} reference
after at most one scheme word. It prints no header value. It also checks that the Triage board exists and anchors its worktrees at the
default repository, that the source checkout vendors the triage playbook that
claude-session gives triage cards, and that the triage refs file is as fresh as
the owned refs. It checks that the default board cannot take the worker slots triage needs
(kanban.max_spawn plus triage.max_running within kanban.max_in_progress, and triage.max_running
within kanban.max_spawn) and reports a triage card that has stayed assigned in ready, with no worker,
for 30 minutes or more. It reports a triage card blocked with kind needs_input or in Hermes' triage status
for it, which park-waiting moves to scheduled, and a scheduled triage card whose newest park has a comment
from blocker-order saying its round was not posted. When any difference is
reported and the source checkout is not at origin/HEAD, the last line names
that checkout. It gives the fast-forward command only when the checkout is
behind, on the default branch, and clean. --ref turns the line off, because
the target is then not origin/HEAD. --print-source prints the checkout path
and exits.

It also checks that watch-gateway, the launchd job that restarts a gateway whose secrets did not
resolve, runs: its script and restart-gateway match the repository, its job is loaded, its
state file is fresh and does not say the restart budget is spent, and the heartbeat URL file exists with
mode 600. It prints no URL.

On macOS, when `setup-broker-user` has written the broker's config, it checks
that the broker runs as its own user: the account and group, who owns each of
its directories, that its root-owned copy of the code matches the commit, its
LaunchDaemon, the root home's socket link, that it holds the GitHub App key
and the factory user cannot read it, judged by ownership and mode only, and
that the gateway runs no broker of its own.

It also checks that Hermes still registers the tools the installed blocker-order plugin watches, so a
Hermes upgrade that renames one is reported. It runs hermes-tool-names with the store Python on the
checkout and the selected environment's site-packages, with a scratch HERMES_HOME and no bytecode, and
never through the `hermes` launcher, which can rebuild environments. It prints a row when Hermes cannot be
imported. HERMES_AGENT_DIR overrides the checkout, which defaults to <root home>/hermes-agent.

It checks that each repository plugin installed in a home is enabled there, from `hermes plugins list
--user --json`, and imports each installed plugin the way Hermes does with import-plugins, in the same
store Python, so a plugin Hermes would skip or fail to load is reported. Hermes only logs both.

On macOS, while no auto-deploy run is alive, it reports a missing or unreadable <root>/auto-deploy.json, a
stale run lock, kanban.dispatch_profiles set in the root home, a hermes pause and a factory-gateway.lock that a
dead run left, a gateway restart that auto-deploy recorded and that has not succeeded, and a default-branch commit that auto-deploy has not deployed a day after it was committed. The
stranded triage check is silent while auto-deploy holds the cards.

--json prints the same drift as a JSON list of records, each with the remedy that install_plan.py names:
an install command and its step, a manual action, or advisory. The text output is the records' lines.

LAUNCHD_ROOT and LAUNCHCTL_BIN point the launchd and broker user checks at
another file system root and launchctl, for tests.
"""
